{
  "rationale": "Evidence: iteration 1 finished 13 of 20 oval trials (mean finish 1.73 s), but all 20 s-bend and all 20 slalom trials left the track. Max error (0.064 to 0.066 m) exceeds the off-track limit of about 0.0575 m, while RMS error stays low (0.03). The robot drifts outward on the harder bends and loses the line 1 to 2 times per run. Steering oscillation (0.15 to 0.21) is moderate, so this is a correction-lag problem, not a runaway oscillation. Body width is already at its maximum (0.18 m), so the off-track margin cannot be widened.\n\nChanges: (1) base_speed 1.2 -> 1.1. This gives the controller more time on tight bends. Oval finished at 1.2 with about 0.27 s of slack, so a small reduction should still finish. (2) kp 4.0 -> 4.5 for a stronger correction at the edge of the line. (3) kd 0.6 -> 0.8 to damp the larger kp. (4) Sensor span widened from x = +/-0.03 to +/-0.04 at 0.02 m spacing. This keeps at least one sensor on the 25 mm tape at larger offsets, which should cut line-loss events and keep the signal at full scale on the outer sensors. (5) ki 0.2 and mass 0.6 kg are unchanged so the drift correction and yaw inertia stay as tested.\n\nExpected result: the s-bend and slalom should start to finish or at least reach much higher progress (above 0.5), and line-loss events should fall toward 0 to 0.5. Oval completion should be retained with a finish time near 1.8 to 2.0 s. Hypotheses to check: (H1) the wider sensor span reduces line-loss events, and the drop in max_error follows from it. (H2) kp 4.5 with kd 0.8 stays stable, so steering_oscillation remains below about 0.35. (H3) base_speed 1.1 still completes the oval within 3 s. If steering_oscillation rises sharply, reduce kp to about 4.0. If the robot survives but does not finish, raise base_speed in steps of 0.1 while watching max_error.",
